{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"c classes and objects c classes and objects","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","fileName":"C# Classes and Objects.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"C# Classes and Objects","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[C Sharp Object-Oriented Type Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Object-Oriented Type Design","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"c classes and objects c classes and objects","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","fileName":"C# Classes and Objects.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"C# Classes and Objects","heading":"C# Classes and Objects","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A C# class defines a reference type whose members describe stored state and supported behavior. An object is an instance created from that type, with its own instance fields and properties while sharing the class's method definitions.\n\nEvery class ultimately derives from `System.Object`, which supplies common behavior such as string representation and type identity. A class acts as a design for possible objects; construction turns that design into a particular value with initialized state.\n\nA class acts as a reusable definition of state and behavior; an object is one instantiated identity created from that definition. Modeling begins by selecting the properties and methods relevant to the application's purpose rather than copying every characteristic of a real-world thing.","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Object-Oriented Type Design","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"c classes and objects c classes and objects","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/C# Classes and Objects.md","fileName":"C# Classes and Objects.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"C# Classes and Objects","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf]]\n\n[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["C Sharp Object-Oriented Type Design","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}